  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual’s practice, an invented shape receives the same prima facie acceptance starting point as an invented word, rather than being disadvantaged simply because it is a shape.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/6~1",
          "quote": "An invented shape should be treated in a similar manner to an invented word, and is equally entitled to prima facie acceptance."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/2",
          "quote": "As such, it is not appropriate to object prima facie that a shape is not functioning as a trade mark merely because it is a shape trade mark and the public will need to be educated to see it as a badge of origin."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "That starting point is not an assurance of registration: the Manual still requires assessment of the shape’s capacity to distinguish in its particular market using the usual tests.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/2",
          "quote": "As with all trade marks, a shape must be considered in the context of the market in which it is placed, and a decision on its adaptation to distinguish then made using the usual tests."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For applicants, what counts as an invented shape depends on the relevant trade, so examination starts with what is common in that trade rather than treating unusual appearance alone as decisive.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/6~1",
          "quote": "Kenman Kandy gives some guidance on what may be regarded as invention in the confectionery trade, but does not offer clear guidance on what amounts to invention in other trades."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/6~1",
          "quote": "A starting point for all trade marks including “shape” trade marks is the question “what is common to the trade”? This can be assessed through research into the shape applied for in the relevant trade."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Research showing several parties using the shape otherwise than as a trade mark tends, under the Manual, to support a ground for rejection.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/6~1",
          "quote": "If research shows a number of parties using a shape in a non-trade mark sense, this will tend to support a view that the marketplace does not regard it as a trade mark and a ground for rejection is in order."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Conversely, a shape need not be entirely new to be inherently capable of distinguishing if it is sufficiently unusual for the particular goods.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/6~1",
          "quote": "In some other classes of goods, while a shape per se might not strictly speaking be a new or different shape, it may be unusual enough in respect of the particular goods of interest to be inherently capable of distinguishing."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Attractiveness alone is not an objection; the Manual’s test is whether other traders are likely to want to use the shape in their ordinary business without an improper motive.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/5",
          "quote": "There is no reason to object to a shape merely because it is attractive or aesthetically pleasing, and therefore other traders might want to use it."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/5",
          "quote": "The test for a shape mark remains the same test applied for any other mark - whether the shape is one which other traders are likely to want to use, in the ordinary course of their business, without any improper motive."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Functional features remain relevant because competitors may need them, although additional features can favour acceptance where the applicant is plainly not seeking or gaining rights in the functional aspects.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/4~1",
          "quote": "If a shape has significant functional features there is a strong likelihood that other traders will need to use that shape for their similar goods."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/4~1",
          "quote": "The presence of functional features need not be fatal to an application. In some cases, the other features of a mark may be sufficient to allow for acceptance where it is obvious that the applicant is not seeking or gaining rights in functional aspects."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For decorative goods such as ornaments or jewellery, the Manual warns that imaginative shapes are normal and applicants will need to demonstrate trade mark use, rather than merely production of an item in that shape.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/3/3/6~1",
          "quote": "In a similar vein, the shapes of decorative items such as ornaments or jewellery will be unlikely to be acceptable prima facie because it is normal within these trades to provide unusual and imaginative creations. The applicant will need to be able to demonstrate that it is using the shape in a trade mark sense, rather than just producing an item in that shape, before it can be accepted for possible registration."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "These conclusions describe the supplied Manual practice, not a statutory rule established by the supplied legislation, which concerns renewal and trade marks attorney or agent offences rather than shape registrability; the materials do not provide a complete account of registration requirements.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
